The  command  can  be used to control the scheduling of processes
and threads.  It  also  returns  information  about  the  current
scheduling  parameters of the process or thread.  Only the super‐
user may change the scheduling parameters.  can also be  used  to
start  a  new  command using the specified parameters.  Available
options: Set of the processors on which process or thread  should
run,  that  is,  affinity.   Processors  are  defined  as numbers
(starting from zero) and separated by commas.  A value of  -1  is
used  to  unset the affinity.  Scheduling class (policy), one of:
Time‐sharing (TS) scheduling policy.  The default policy in First
in, first out (FIFO) scheduling policy.   Round‐robin  scheduling
policy.   Priority for the process or thread.  Value should be in
the range from (0) to (63).  Setting of priority for the  process
or  thread  running at policy is not allowed.  The target process
which will be affected.  If the process has more than one thread,
all of them will be affected.  If is not given, a command to exe‐
cute must be given on the command line.  Thread in the  specified
process.   If  specified, only this thread in the process will be
affected.  May only be specified if is also given.  Show schedul‐
ing information about the process whose ID is # schedctl  ‐p  123
